## Bundled runtime libraries

AirPlayEngine's C target links against these libraries at build/run time
via Homebrew (see AirPlayEngine/Package.swift). In a release/distribution
build (`scripts/make-app.sh` run with `AUDIOUT_BUNDLE_DYLIBS=1`), these
libraries are copied into `Contents/Frameworks/` inside the shipped
`.app` so it can run without a Homebrew install present. A plain local
dev build (the flag unset) still links them from the developer's own
Homebrew install and does not bundle anything.

Because libgcrypt, libgpg-error, and libplist are
LGPL-2.1-or-later, and ffmpeg may be LGPL-2.1-or-later or
GPL-2.0-or-later depending on build configuration, bundling these
libraries into the app does trigger LGPL redistribution obligations
that did not apply when they were only dynamically loaded from a
user-managed Homebrew install.

The compliance mechanism used for the dynamically-linked libraries:
each library ships as its own separate, individually replaceable
`.dylib` file inside `Contents/Frameworks/` — never statically merged
into AirPlayEngine's or Audiout's own binary. This is intended to
satisfy LGPL §6's requirement that a user be able to relink or
substitute a compatible version of the library. This is not a legal
guarantee; it is the mechanism this project relies on for compliance.

ffmpeg — minimal, audio-only, and (in a release build) statically
linked. AirPlayEngine only ever asks ffmpeg to ENCODE captured PCM to
Apple Lossless (ALAC); it never decodes video or any media file. A
release build therefore replaces Homebrew's full ffmpeg with a minimal
audio-only build produced by `scripts/build-min-ffmpeg.sh` (configured
`--disable-everything --enable-encoder=alac --enable-swresample`, no
`--enable-gpl` and no GPL-only components — so it is LGPL-2.1-or-later).
This minimal ffmpeg is linked STATICALLY into AirPlayEngine, which means
its transitive video-codec dependencies (x264, x265, libvpx, dav1d,
SvtAv1Enc) and unused audio codecs (lame, opus) — and the OpenSSL
(libcrypto/libssl) that Homebrew's network-enabled ffmpeg pulls in — are
NO LONGER present in the shipped app at all. Because ffmpeg is
statically linked here rather than shipped as a replaceable `.dylib`,
the LGPL §6 relink obligation is satisfied on a different basis: the
whole app is GPL-2.0-or-later with complete corresponding source
available (including `scripts/build-min-ffmpeg.sh`, which reproducibly
builds the exact ffmpeg from pinned upstream source), so a user can
rebuild and re-link the entire work — which meets and exceeds LGPL §6's
source-availability alternative to the shared-library mechanism. A GPL
build of ffmpeg would be license-compatible too, since the whole app is
already GPL-2.0-or-later; bundling ffmpeg introduces no GPL/LGPL
conflict either way. If the minimal build is absent, AirPlayEngine falls
back to linking Homebrew's ffmpeg dylibs, which are then bundled by the
`otool -L` walk (the fat behaviour described below).

Libraries, listed for attribution and so contributors know what to
`brew install` for a dev build:

- libevent (BSD-3-Clause)
- libsodium (ISC)
- libgcrypt, libgpg-error (LGPL-2.1-or-later)
- libplist (LGPL-2.1-or-later)
- ffmpeg (LGPL-2.1-or-later; minimal audio-only build) — libavcodec
  (ALAC encoder only), libavutil, libswresample

Note: when the fat Homebrew ffmpeg fallback is used, the exact set of
files that land in `Contents/Frameworks/` — especially ffmpeg's
transitive dependencies (libraries such as x264, aom, dav1d, or others
depending on how ffmpeg was built) — is determined by an `otool -L`
dependency walk performed at build time by `scripts/make-app.sh`, and
can vary with the Homebrew version used to build. This NOTICE describes
the top-level libraries and the bundling mechanism; the authoritative
exact file list for any given build is whatever is actually present in
that build's `Contents/Frameworks/`, not a list frozen here.
